That ruleset supplied the groundwork this one is built on: the Lean rules, the Lake
integration, the RulesLean introspection library, and the tree-shaking hook. Most of the
code here is still theirs, and the design owes it a great deal.

The import is recorded as its own commit, unmodified, so that everything added since is
visible as a plain diff against the original.

WHY A SEPARATE RULESET
----------------------

The four changes below alter how the Lake workspace is fetched, when it is fetched, and
which toolchain an action receives. Together they reach into the core of the Lake
integration and the toolchain model. Work of that size is awkward to carry as a growing
patch set against another project, and it benefits from its own version and release
cadence, so it is published here instead. Where a change proves useful upstream, it is
offered back.

WHAT THIS RULESET ADDS
----------------------

1. Content-addressed fetch (`lock`)

   A Lake workspace is normally resolved at fetch time: `lake update` clones every package
   and `lake exe cache get` pulls one blob per Mathlib module, both inside `rctx.execute`.
   Bazel's repository cache and `--experimental_remote_downloader` key on sha256 and
   cannot see into a subprocess, so the workspace is re-fetched on every cold output base
   and the tree it produces is not byte-reproducible.

   Adds a lockfile pinning every package, and the prebuilt olean archive, by URL and
   sha256, so the workspace materializes with `download_and_extract` and nothing else.

2. Downloadless toolchain declarations

   Registering a toolchain makes Bazel fetch the repository holding the `toolchain()`
   target, so resolution can evaluate it, and resolution runs for every target in every
   build. With the declaration inside the Lake workspace repository, a build that compiled
   no Lean still materialized the entire workspace.

   Emits the declarations into a repository that downloads nothing.

3. Per-execution-platform toolchain selection

   A repository rule always runs on the host, so host detection cannot answer which
   platform an action will execute on. An unconstrained `toolchain()` matches every
   execution platform, so under remote execution the host's binary is sent to the worker
   and fails with an exec-format error.

   Declares one toolchain per execution platform, constrained, and fetches only the one
   that is selected.

4. Reproducible olean archive packer (`tools/`)

   Supporting tool for the lockfile path: cuts the artifact set to a consumer's import
   closure and packs it into a byte-reproducible archive.
